WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.666
EtherNet/IP and PROFINET are prominent industrial Ethernet technologies,

00:00:03.666 --> 00:00:09.460
but they are not simply ordinary office applications placed on a plant network.

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:13.471
They support device discovery, configuration, diagnostics, cyclic control data,

00:00:13.471 --> 00:00:18.820
and other operations whose timing and availability may be important to production.

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:22.386
EtherNet/IP uses the Common Industrial Protocol, or CIP,

00:00:22.386 --> 00:00:25.060
across standard Ethernet and IP networking.

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:31.746
It can carry recurring input and output data as well as explicit messages for configuration,

00:00:31.746 --> 00:00:33.529
status, and other services.

00:00:33.529 --> 00:00:39.323
The expected device roles and connection patterns depend on the engineered control solution.

00:00:39.323 --> 00:00:43.780
PROFINET supports communication among controllers, distributed input and output, drives,

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:45.563
and related automation equipment.

00:00:45.563 --> 00:00:51.357
Different communication classes can serve ordinary supervisory functions and more demanding real-time control.

00:00:51.357 --> 00:00:57.597
Engineering and discovery functions are useful operationally but should remain within approved administrative pathways.

00:00:57.597 --> 00:01:00.717
Both protocols exist inside broader vendor ecosystems.

00:01:00.717 --> 00:01:05.174
Controllers, safety systems, HMIs, drives, engineering suites, firmware, device profiles,

00:01:05.174 --> 00:01:08.740
and management tools are designed to work together.

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:13.197
Security teams need vendor and site documentation because product versions,

00:01:13.197 --> 00:01:15.871
optional protections, and supported configurations vary.

00:01:15.871 --> 00:01:18.100
Real-time requirements shape defensive work.

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:22.557
Unplanned scanning, inline controls, or configuration changes can introduce latency,

00:01:22.557 --> 00:01:25.231
device stress, or loss of communication.

00:01:25.231 --> 00:01:31.471
Assess changes in a representative environment and coordinate deployment with control engineers and vendors.

00:01:31.471 --> 00:01:36.374
Protocol security often depends on architecture and administration: segment control zones,

00:01:36.374 --> 00:01:41.277
restrict engineering tools, control discovery and management paths, secure remote access,

00:01:41.277 --> 00:01:45.734
preserve known-good configurations, and monitor unexpected devices or role changes.

00:01:45.734 --> 00:01:47.517
Protocol awareness identifies behavior;

00:01:47.517 --> 00:01:52.420
engineering context determines whether that behavior is necessary, approved, and safe.
